    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by exploring the existing codebase to understand the structure and conventions.\nLet me list the directory structure:\nNow let me read the key files to understand the structure:\nNow let me check the protocol package to understand how to register routes:\nNow let me check the action package to understand the Executor interface:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity structure:\nNow let me check the cmd package to see how the main service is structured:\nNow I have a good understanding of the codebase. Let me implement the reopen action. Based on the requirements and existing code structure, I need to:\n\n1. Add the `reopenOp` operation to `operations.go`\n2. Create an `ItemHandler` for the reopen action in `approval.go`\n3. Register the new route with `protocol.Item` in the `New` function\n\nLet's start:\nNow let's update the approval.go file to add the reopen handler:\nNow let's build and test the code:\nGreat! The build succeeded. Now let's run the tests to make sure everything works:\nPerfect! Let me verify that my implementation is correct by reviewing what I've done:\nPerfect! Let me verify the implementation against the requirements:\n\n## Implementation Summary\n\nI've successfully added the reopen action to the existing service. Here's what was done:\n\n### 1. **Operation Declaration** (`operations.go`)\nAdded `reopenOp` with the following properties:\n- **Name**: \"reopen.v5\" - matches the requirement for operation name in ActionRequest\n- **Role**: \"requester\" - only requesters can call this endpoint\n- **Own**: true - can only reopen their own purchases\n- **From**: \"rejected\" - only purchases in rejected status can be reopened\n- **To**: \"submitted\" - status changes to submitted after successful reopen\n- **Field**: \"\" - single-target operation (not a batch), response format is `{\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`\n- **Event**: `action.NoEvent` - no outbox event needed per spec\n\n### 2. **Handler Implementation** (`approval.go`)\n- Created `reopen()` function that returns a `protocol.ItemHandler`\n- The handler calls `executor.Run()` with the reason parameter\n- Returns `ItemResult` with id and status=\"submitted\"\n- Properly adapts `action.Rejection` errors to `protocol.Rejection` at the boundary\n\n### 3. **Route Registration** (`approval.go`)\n- Updated `New()` function to wrap the batch routes with `protocol.Item()`\n- Registers path pattern: `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen`\n- Associates with the \"requester\" role\n- Chains with existing batch routes via the fallback handler\n\n### 4. **Database & RLS Support**\nThe implementation leverages existing infrastructure:\n- SCHEMA.sql v5 already has RLS policies configured for requesters to update their own rejected purchases back to submitted\n- The guarded RLS prevents unauthorized access\n- `ApprovalAudit` table now accepts \"reopen.v5\" operations and tracks the reason field\n- `ActionRequest` table accepts the \"reopen.v5\" operation for idempotency\n\n### 5. **Protocol Layer Integration**\nUses `protocol.Item` which:\n- Validates the request body as `{\"reason\":\"...\"}` with 1-200 byte reason\n- Binds the authenticated identity with required role check\n- Validates Idempotency-Key header\n- Handles idempotency with replay detection\n- Returns 4xx on validation failures, 409 on concurrent conflicts\n- Returns 503 on ActionRequest write failures (with no database changes)\n- Preserves bulk-approve and bulk-reject APIs unchanged\n\nThe implementation follows existing code conventions and is fully integrated with the platform Action kernel for transactional semantics, idempotency, RLS, and audit logging."
